  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1023 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAn upper low currently evident in satellite imagery over the\nPacific NW will be the driver of a potential mid-week front and \nassociated precip chances. In advance of this front, the upper low\nemerging onto the northern Plains will act to flatten and confine\nthe upper ridge to the southern Plains. Despite this, the ridge \nstrengthens during the Tues-Wed timeframe, with 500mb values \nincreasing to the 596-598 dam range. Such values are approaching \nthe local max of the model climatology for this time of year, \nwhich lends support to the near-record to record highs delivered \nby the model blend. The most likely day for potential record heat \nis Wed (9/9), which happens to have cooler record highs than the \nsurrounding days. The record highs for Wed in Lubbock and \nChildress are 99F and 102F, respectively, and the latest forecast \nvalues are 97F and 102F. \n\nIn response to that upper disturbance, a sfc low will track across\nthe Canadian Prairie, while a sprawling cold front arcs well \nsouthward onto the central Plains by Wed. Latest guidance suggests\nthe front will still mainly be north of our area on Wed, keeping \nthe precip chances (20-30%) primarily across the TX Panhandle. By \nThurs, the center of the upper ridge shifts towards the Four \nCorners, placing West Texas in NW flow aloft as models nudge the \nfront south into our FA. The presence of this front and the \nslightly more favorable upper conditions support mentionable \nprecip on Thurs (20- 30% chance). Despite the potential for \nprecip, the model blend keeps the hot, above normal temps in place\nfor Thurs (and into the weekend). \n\nLate in the forecast period, attention turns to the evolution of \nyet another upper low over the Pacific NW. While there is \npotential for a noticeable cool down in west Texas early next \nweek, considerable spread exists regarding the timing and strength\nof this system. As such, forecast confidence beyond Saturday is \nlower than usual for the Days 7-10 range (Sep 13-16). One way to \nquantify this uncertainty is by looking at the the NBM 25th-75th \npercentile spread. For the high temperatures, this spread is 10-15\ndegF (low 80s to mid 90s for Lubbock). This tells us that 50% of \nthe individual members have high temps outside of this already \nbroad range. It will be an intriguing period of the forecast to \nmonitor and see how the ensemble guidance evolves over the next \nfew days.",
